Chapter: LirJoinCross

 The join-shaped `if`: one whose arms do not return but compute into a shared
 vreg and jump to a join block, where the joined value is used. This is the
 shape `LiMove` and `LiJump` admit (LirRetarget step 4g), and it is distinct
 from the returning `if` of `lir-branch-cross`, whose arms each `LiRet`.

 An `if` reaches this shape when it is in NON-tail position -- its value feeds
 surrounding code -- so each arm ends `LiMove v_s <arm>; LiJump join` and the
 join carries the result. `let v = if ... in v` leaves the join block empty;
 `(if ...) + 1` puts the continuation in it.

 Pinned by ANSWERS, because the plug and x86-64 reach them by different
 instructions and the answers are what must agree. The sensitivity proof that
 the ARM64 LIR path actually emits these -- rather than the tree emitter behind
 it -- is a perturbation of `a64-lir-emit-mov`, run once by hand: corrupting it
 breaks exactly these rows and nothing else in the ARM64 battery.

Section: Shapes

 An empty-exit join: the joined value is the whole result, so the join block
 carries nothing and the arms fall out the bottom into the epilogue.

  sel-let : Integer -> Integer
  sel-let (c) =
   let v = if c > 0 then 3 else 4
   in v

 A join with a continuation in the exit block: the `+ 1` lands in the join
 rather than leaving it empty.

  sel-add : Integer -> Integer
  sel-add (c) =
   (if c > 0 then 10 else 20) + 1

 A join whose arms are parameters, not immediates, so each `LiMove` copies a
 register rather than materialising a literal.

  sel-reg : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sel-reg (c) (x) (y) =
   let v = if c > 0 then x else y
   in v * 2

 A nested join: the inner `if` is itself non-tail, feeding the outer arm.

  sel-nest : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sel-nest (a) (b) =
   (if a > 0 then (if b > 0 then 1 else 2) else 3) + 10

 Two independent joins in sequence, so a second `LiMove`/`LiJump` pair follows
 the first's join block.

  sel-seq : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  sel-seq (a) (b) =
   let p = if a > 0 then 5 else 6
   in let q = if b > 0 then 7 else 8
   in p * 10 + q

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   print-line-uni (show (sel-let 1))
   print-line-uni (show (sel-let (-1)))
   print-line-uni (show (sel-add 1))
   print-line-uni (show (sel-add (-1)))
   print-line-uni (show (sel-reg 1 30 40))
   print-line-uni (show (sel-reg (-1) 30 40))
   print-line-uni (show (sel-nest 1 1))
   print-line-uni (show (sel-nest 1 (-1)))
   print-line-uni (show (sel-nest (-1) 5))
   print-line-uni (show (sel-seq 1 1))
   print-line-uni (show (sel-seq 1 (-1)))
   print-line-uni (show (sel-seq (-1) (-1)))
  end
